    "case_id": "687",
    "case_summary": "A 2-year-old girl was admitted with 2-day history of fever, severe sore throat, neck pain, vomiting and poor intake. She looked fatigue with moderate dehydration. She had no significant past medical history.\n\nPhysical findings included:- High-grade fever (body temperature 39.9°C)- Pharyngeal erythema- Enlarged tonsils with whitish tonsillar exudates- No lymphadenopathy or hepatosplenomegaly on palpation- Stiff neck on all directions- Negative kernig's and brudzinski's signs- Initially unremarkable other neurological findings- Later developed altered consciousness and generalized tonic-clonic seizures lasting 1 minute- Post-ictal drowsiness and unresponsiveness to verbal commands\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Hemoglobin: 12.9 g/dL- Leukocyte count: 10,170 cells/mm3 (lymphocyte 51%, neutrophil 41%, monocyte 8%)- Platelet count: 239,000 cells/mm3- ESR: 6 mm/hr- Procalcitonin: 0.20 ng/mL- Normal liver transaminases- CSF analysis: leukocyte 250 cells/mm3 (neutrophil 20%, lymphocyte 80%), glucose 58 mg/dL, protein 35 mg/dLSerology:- Positive viral capsid antigen (VCA) IgM- Negative VCA IgG- Absent Epstein-Barr nuclear antigen (EBNA)- Positive EBV DNA in CSFImaging Studies:- CT head/neck findings: Diffuse leptomeningeal enhancement along bilateral parietal cortical sulci and around brainstem, small hypodense lesion in left high parietal lobe, bilateral tonsillitis and multiple cervical lymphadenopathies- MRI findings: Regression of leptomeningeal enhancement along brainstem surface, circumferential T2/FLAIR hyperintense rim at midbrain and pons, subacute cortical infarction at parasagittal bilateral parietal lobes, thin subdural hemorrhage along left temporooccipital convexities",
